"Book_of_the_Dead": {"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/eg/original/36.3.2_EGDP013731.jpg","title":"Heart scarab of Hatnefer","desc":"The inscribed underside of a heart scarab, a green serpentinite oval mounted in gold on a gold chain and covered with about a dozen lines of incised hieroglyphs. Such amulets were laid over the breast of the dead and carry a spell of the Book of the Dead addressed to the heart. New Kingdom, Dynasty 18, about 1491 to 1473 BCE; serpentinite and gold, the scarab 6.6 cm (2 5/8 in.) long. Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, Rogers Fund, 1936 (36.3.2).","credit":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, accession 36.3.2, public domain (CC0), https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/545146","alt":"A dark green oval stone set in a plain gold rim and hung from a long looped gold chain. The stone's flat face is engraved with roughly a dozen ruled lines of small hieroglyphs."},
