"Chinese_flood_myth": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg/1280px-%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg","title":"Yu ordering the water works","desc":"Woodblock illustration from the Ming novel Youxia zhizhuan, 17th century, digitised in the Guben xiaoshuo jicheng. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg","alt":"Line woodcut in a circular frame: a seated ruler under a draped canopy gestures toward three kneeling officials, the panel title running down each side in characters."},
"Chinese_legendary_period": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/%E6%AD%A6%E6%A2%81%E7%A5%A0%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E7%9F%B3.jpg","title":"A carved stone of the Wu Liang shrine","desc":"An ink rubbing of one of the carved stones of the Wu Liang shrine in Shandong, a Han family shrine of the 2nd century CE, reproduced in Édouard Chavannes's archaeological survey of northern China. The record names the stone and not the figures carved on it. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%AD%A6%E6%A2%81%E7%A5%A0%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E7%9F%B3.jpg","alt":"An ink rubbing printed white on dark grey, of a stone slab with a gabled top. The gable carries a frieze of animals and small figures above a band of scrollwork. Below it runs a row of standing robed figures, each framed in its own narrow upright compartment under a scalloped canopy. Two further bands beneath hold seated figures, horses and carriages."},
