"Ionic_order": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Vitruvius_the_Ten_Books_on_Architecture_-_The_Ionic_Order_according_to_Vitruvius.png","title":"The Ionic order, drawn to Vitruvius's proportions","desc":"A plate from Morris Hicky Morgan's 1914 translation of Vitruvius, captioned \"The Ionic order according to Vitruvius compared with the order of the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus\" and noting the difference between the Roman and the Greek relation of the baluster-side of the capital to the echinus. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vitruvius_the_Ten_Books_on_Architecture_-_The_Ionic_Order_according_to_Vitruvius.png","alt":"A line drawing of two Ionic orders side by side, each a fluted column on a moulded base under a capital of spiral volutes carrying an architrave of three overlapping bands; the left is closed by a plain sculptured frieze, the right by a row of small blocks, with a compass diagram of the volute's spiral between them."},
